ent seven months through January 1978.\n\nThe total outstanding in January 1977 was $164,005 million, which obviously exceeds $20,000 million. This suggests a significant financial contraction in Treasury bill issuances during the mid-to-late 1977 period.\n\nBy January 1978, the total outstanding stands at $161,221 million, indicating a slight recovery but still reflecting the lower issuance pattern observed in the preceding months.\n\nI need to verify the interpretation of the data, focusing on the precise meaning of \"total nominal outstanding\" in the Treasury bulletin. The table's structure provides key insights into how the total amount is calculated, showing the distribution across different investor categories.\n\nThe \"Total amount outstanding\" represents the comprehensive sum of all Treasury securities, which can indeed exceed simple addition of individual monthly issues. This suggests complex aggregation beyond straightforward monthly issue totals.\n\nI'll carefully track the total outstanding for Treasury bills, recognizing potential discrepancies between individual issue amounts and the aggregate total. My analysis requires cross-referencing both 1977 and 1978 data to understand the full picture of Treasury bill outstanding.\n\nThe key is to methodically examine how the total outstanding changes across different reporting periods, ensuring precise interpretation of the financial data.\n\nI'll focus on calculating the number of months where Treasury bills exceed $20,000 million, carefully mapping the 24-month period from February 1977 to January 1979.\n\nMy approach involves systematically reviewing available data points: January 1977 shows $164,005 million, which clearly meets the threshold. The subsequent months require detailed tracking to determine precise monthly variations.\n\nThe challenge lies in the limited data snapshots, with only two recorded ownership surveys available. This constraint significantly complicates determining exact monthly outs